---
import Pipeline from "./Pipeline.astro";
import AskKick from "./AskKick.astro";
import PartHead from "./PartHead.astro";
import NextPart from "./NextPart.astro";
import Beat from "./Beat.astro";
import Touchline from "./Touchline.astro";
import AiStudy from "./AiStudy.astro";
import Recount from "./Recount.astro";
import { framePrompt } from "../../lib/primer";
import { scheduleLog, noPublishedScore } from "../../lib/first-run";
import { bounds, story, kickGrid } from "../../lib/coverage";
import { pipeline, spell, toPlay } from "../../lib/cases";

const sources = [
  ["pages", "The matches listed on the pages Kick saved"],
  ["log", "The lines in Kick's fetch log"],
  ["rule", "The membership list and the competition's format"],
];
const bar = (n: number) => `${(n / story.scheduled) * 100}%`;
const chain = [
  [story.scheduled, "scheduled by the format", ""],
  [story.finals, "finished by Friday", `${toPlay} dated after Friday`],
  [story.finals - bounds.unseen, "on saved hosts' pages", `${bounds.unseen} Dunmore home matches: Kick read each match only from its host's page, and Dunmore's didn't answer`],
  [story.counted, "counted in Kick's table", `${noPublishedScore} finished match with no score on the page (Case 3)`],
] as const;
---

<section id="case-dunmore">
  <PartHead id="case-dunmore">Case 1. Dunmore: a count needs an expected set</PartHead>
  <Pipeline small lit="scrape" note="This case happens at Scrape" />

  <p class="cv-callout">Dunmore athletics: “You've got our men at six conference games. We've played thirteen.”</p>
  <p>Part 2 asked how many matches had finished by Friday. Dunmore knows its own number. Kick's table had no way to show that six was short.</p>

  <Beat part="case-dunmore" name="Look" title="What the fetch log shows" />
  <p>Besides the table, Kick kept a log of every page it requested. Here are the {spell(pipeline.asked)} schedule page requests:</p>
  <div class="cv-agent-reply" role="region" aria-label="Kick's fetch log">
    <div class="cv-chat-bar">
      <span class="cv-agent-badge">Kick · Friday's fetch log</span>
      <span class="cv-status-note">{pipeline.saved} saved · {pipeline.silent} no answer</span>
    </div>
    <div class="cv-agent-record">
      <table class="cv-log">
        <caption class="sr-only">Kick's fetch log, schedule pages</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">School</th><th scope="col">Fetch status</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{scheduleLog.map((l) => <tr class:list={{ "is-gap": l.result !== "saved" }}><th scope="row">{l.name}</th><td>{l.result}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>The missing page sits right there in one line of the log, yet Kick's summary never mentions it. Try asking Kick a follow-up question:</p>
  <AskKick />

  <h4 class="cv-try">How many matches were scheduled?</h4>
  <p>Kick's log names the missing page, but it can't tell you how many matches went missing with it, because nothing in the run records how many matches should exist. To measure the gap in matches, first work out how many the conference scheduled.</p>
  <div class="cv-check" data-once="scheduled">
    <p class="cv-kind">Knowledge check</p>
    <div class="cv-line" data-q data-type="select" data-answer="rule" data-why="The membership list and the competition format. Both exist before the run and independently of it, so a school that never answers stays in the count." data-wrong={JSON.stringify({ pages: "Here the saved pages happen to list all 56, but only because seven of eight answered. A count taken from the run can confirm the expected set, never establish it: the more pages fail, the less it lists.", log: "The log only counts pages requested, not matches." })}>
      <label><span>Where can the expected set of matches come from, before the run?</span>
        <select data-save="q-source"><option value="">Choose</option>{sources.map(([k, t]) => <option value={k}>{t}</option>)}</select>
      </label>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <div data-q data-type="number" data-answer={story.scheduled} data-why={`${story.scheduled}. ${spell(story.schools, true)} hosts, each with ${spell(story.schools - 1)} visitors: ${story.schools} × ${story.schools - 1}. You don't need to fetch a single page to know that.`}>
      <label for="cv-scheduled">Each of the {spell(story.schools)} schools hosts every other school once. How many conference matches are in the full season?</label>
      <input id="cv-scheduled" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" data-save="q-scheduled" />
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <p class="cv-once">Answer both, then check.</p>
    <button type="button" data-submit>Check answers</button>
    <p class="cv-fb" role="status" data-need></p>
    <div class="cv-reveal"><p>Between them, the {spell(pipeline.saved)} saved schedules list all {story.scheduled} pairings, and they date {spell(toPlay)} for Saturday, November 21, the day after Friday's run. One of those two is Dunmore's, which is why Dunmore has played thirteen, not fourteen.</p></div>
  </div>

  <Beat part="case-dunmore" name="Name it" title="The sampling frame" />
  <p>Kick had a list of pages, which is why its log could name Dunmore. It had no list of matches, so its {story.counted} matches were compared against nothing. An expected set of matches, taken from the conference format, is the reference that turns that count into coverage.</p>
  <p>Survey statistics has names for these pieces. The membership list is the <strong>sampling frame</strong>: the list of units you collect from, here the {spell(kickGrid.length)} schools. The frame plus the conference format gives the <strong>expected observations</strong>, which this module calls the expected set. When a unit on the frame never answers, that is <strong>unit nonresponse</strong>. Dunmore's schedule page never answered, so Friday has one case of it.</p>
  <dl class="cv-terms" aria-label="Friday's pieces and their survey statistics names">
    <div><dt>The membership list</dt><dd><b>Sampling frame</b><span>The {spell(kickGrid.length)} schools to collect from</span></dd></div>
    <div><dt>The frame and the format</dt><dd><b>Expected observations</b><span>The {story.scheduled} matches those schools should play</span></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Dunmore's unanswered page</dt><dd><b>Unit nonresponse</b><span>A school on the frame that never answered</span></dd></div>
  </dl>
  <AiStudy
    id="dunmore-ai-study"
    title="Where do these terms come from?"
    note="Send this prompt to read the reply an AI assistant gave, or copy it into your own. Then come back and caption Friday's standings."
    prompt={framePrompt}
    href="../01-collection-ai-frames/"
  />

  <Touchline part="case-dunmore" at={["Fetch"]} note="At Fetch, before the first request." title="Where Touchline gets its expected counts">
    <p>Touchline's expected set of pages comes from a season membership file kept apart from any scraper run, and its expected match totals come from the conference format. Counting whatever appears on saved pages is a weaker fallback, because it is only as complete as the pages that answered.</p>
  </Touchline>

  <Beat part="case-dunmore" name="Your claim" title="Caption Friday's standings" />
  <div class="cv-check" data-once="claim-count">
    <p class="cv-kind">Apply it</p>
    <div data-q data-type="number" data-answer={`${story.counted},${story.finals}`} data-why={`${story.counted} of ${story.finals}. Kick's summary reported ${story.counted}. The bottom of the fraction is what should exist by now: ${story.scheduled} scheduled, minus ${spell(toPlay)} still to play. Not ${story.scheduled} (unplayed matches aren't missing) and not ${story.counted} (a count of what was saved can't show what wasn't).`}>
      <p class="cv-qlabel">Write the caption Kick's table needed. Kick's summary only stated how many matches it counted. The season has {story.scheduled} matches, and {spell(toPlay)} are dated after Friday.</p>
      <p class="cv-row cv-fillin">
        <span>Built from</span>
        <input inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" aria-label="Matches in the table" data-save="claim-count" />
        <span>of</span>
        <input inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" aria-label="Finished conference matches" data-save="claim-total" />
        <span>finished conference matches.</span>
      </p>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <p class="cv-once">Enter both numbers to check and see the full breakdown.</p>
    <button type="button" data-submit>Check caption</button>
    <p class="cv-fb" role="status" data-need></p>
    <div class="cv-reveal">
      <Recount solved />
      <p>Here is where the other {story.scheduled - story.counted} of the {story.scheduled} scheduled matches went.</p>
      <div class="cv-chain" style={`--unplayed:${bar(toPlay)}`} role="img" aria-label={chain.map(([n, what, lost]) => (lost ? `${lost}, leaving ${n} ${what}` : `${n} ${what}`)).join(". ")}>
        {chain.map(([n, what, lost]) => <div>{lost && <p class="cv-drop">minus {lost}</p>}<span class="cv-track"><span style={`width:${bar(n)}`}></span></span><p><b>{n}</b> {what}</p></div>)}
      </div>
      <p>Riley could have known the {story.scheduled}, the {story.finals} and the missing {spell(bounds.unseen)} before publishing. With an expected set, any reader can tell the table is short.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <NextPart from="case-dunmore">Dunmore's was the only site that didn't answer. So why was Hartwell's row wrong?</NextPart>
</section>
